#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:9
msgid "Simulating graded electrodes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:11
msgid ""
"In this notebook we explore how to simulate the effect of graded electrodes "
"in the performance of a battery. Graded electrodes have a composition that "
"varies along the thickness of the electrode, typically active material "
"volume fraction and particle size. This variation can be used to improve the "
"performance of the battery, for example, by increasing the power density."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:13
msgid "As usual, we start by importing PyBaMM."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid "[1]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"%pip install \"pybamm[plot,cite]\" -q # install PyBaMM if it is not "
"installed\n"
"import pybamm"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:35
msgid ""
"<pre>\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"</pre>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:41
msgid ""
"\\begin{sphinxVerbatim}[commandchars=\\\\\\{\\}]\n"
"Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages.\n"
"\\end{sphinxVerbatim}"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:48
msgid "Note: you may need to restart the kernel to use updated packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:59
msgid ""
"We use the DFN model for the simulations and the Chen2020 parameter set. "
"Note that we will need to modify the default Chen2020 parameter set to "
"describe graded electrodes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid "[2]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"model = pybamm.lithium_ion.DFN()\n"
"parameter_values = pybamm.ParameterValues(\"Chen2020\")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:81
msgid ""
"We will vary the porosity in both electrodes and we will try three different "
"scenarios: constant porosity, one where lower porosity occurs near the "
"separator and one where lower porosity occurs near the current collector. "
"All other parameters are kept constant. The varying porosity is defined to "
"be linear centered around the default value and with a variation of :math:"
"`\\pm` 10%."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:83
msgid ""
"We define the varying porosities and store them in a list so we can loop "
"over when solving the model. Note that the porosities must be specified as "
"functions of x, y, and z, even if y and z are not present in the model and "
"not referred to at any other point, as in this example."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid "[3]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"L_n = parameter_values[\"Negative electrode thickness [m]\"]\n"
"L_s = parameter_values[\"Separator thickness [m]\"]\n"
"L_p = parameter_values[\"Positive electrode thickness [m]\"]\n"
"\n"
"eps_n_0 = parameter_values[\"Negative electrode porosity\"]\n"
"eps_p_0 = parameter_values[\"Positive electrode porosity\"]\n"
"\n"
"eps_ns = [\n"
"    eps_n_0,\n"
"    lambda x, y, z: eps_n_0 * (1.1 - 0.2 * (x / L_n)),\n"
"    lambda x, y, z: eps_n_0 * (0.9 + 0.2 * (x / L_n)),\n"
"]\n"
"\n"
"eps_ps = [\n"
"    eps_p_0,\n"
"    lambda x, y, z: eps_p_0 * (0.9 - 0.2 / L_p * (L_n + L_s) + 0.2 * (x / "
"L_p)),\n"
"    lambda x, y, z: eps_p_0 * (1.1 + 0.2 / L_p * (L_n + L_s) - 0.2 * (x / "
"L_p)),\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:121
msgid ""
"Note that the distance through the electrode is computed from the negative "
"electrode, so parameter need to be defined accordingly. Next, we can just "
"solve the models for the various parameter sets. We apply a fairly high C-"
"rate to see the effect of the graded electrodes on the discharge capacity."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid "[4]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"solutions = []\n"
"\n"
"experiment = pybamm.Experiment([\"Discharge at 3C until 2.5 V\"])\n"
"\n"
"for eps_n, eps_p in zip(eps_ns, eps_ps, strict=False):\n"
"    parameter_values[\"Negative electrode porosity\"] = eps_n\n"
"    parameter_values[\"Positive electrode porosity\"] = eps_p\n"
"    sim = pybamm.Simulation(\n"
"        model,\n"
"        parameter_values=parameter_values,\n"
"        experiment=experiment,\n"
"    )\n"
"    sol = sim.solve()\n"
"    solutions.append(sol)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:155
msgid "And plot the results:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid "[5]:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../source/examples/notebooks/models/graded-electrodes.ipynb:-1
msgid ""
"pybamm.dynamic_plot(\n"
"    solutions,\n"
"    labels=[\n"
"        \"Constant porosity\",\n"
"        \"Low porosity at separator\",\n"
"        \"High porosity at separator\",\n"
"    ],\n"
")"
msgstr ""

